Thanks for your reply. But I'm still confusing about it. I will elaborate my model here.

Let y_{itj} stands for the count for area i, year t and subject j, where j ranges from 1 to N_{ij}. So there are different number of subjects on area i. The model in my mind is

y_{itj} ~ Poisson (mu_{itj})
log(mu_{itj}) = alpha + s_i + d_t + e_{itj}

So  e_{itj} ~ N(0, sigma2) will be the individual level error term.
s_i is the spatial effect for area i, where d_t is the temporal effect for year t. In the end, I will add spatial temporal interaction term, but I am omitting it here.

s = (s_1, s_2, ..., s_I) will be a vector of length I (number of areas), which I need to estimate. I want to use, for example, a BYM model.

d = (d_1, ..., d_T) is length T (number of years) year effect, which I want to use RW1 or RW2.

The future spatio-temporal interaction effect will have a precision matrix which is the Kronecker product of the precisions matrix for the spatial and temporal effect. Constraints will be added to ensure identifiability. I am not include it for the moment.

So in this case, I'm assuming the spatial effect s_i, or the year effect d_t, is NOT replicated for each subject on area i and year t. Since for all those subjects on area i and year t, I'm assuming they are sharing the same effect, which is not independent different realizations replicated by a same latent process from the same hyper priors.

If I'm understanding correctly, do I even have to use the replicate argument?
Is the following model correct?
formular = y ~ 1 +
             f(year, model="rw2", hyper = list(prec = list(prior="loggamma", param=c(1,0.005))))+
      f(area, model="bym2", graph=g, scale.model=TRUE, constr=TRUE,
        hyper=list(
          phi=list(prior="pc", param=c(phi.u, phi.alpha), initial = -3),
          prec =list(prior="pc.prec", param=c(u,alpha), initial=5)
       ))+
               f(error, model="iid", hyper = list(prec = list(prior="pc.prec", param=c(u,alpha), initial=5)))+

The data looks like (suppose I have 2 areas, 2 year, and N_{11} = 2, N_{12} = 1, N_{21} = 3, N_{22} = 2 are number of subjects on each area-year combination.)
y       area   year   error
3       1       1       1
5       1       1       2
8       1       2       3
5       2       1       4
6       2       1       5
2       2       1       6
10      2       2       7
2       2       2       8

