If a rw2 model overfits you most likely have an unreasonable prior,
yes. The more common case is
oversmoothing, but then an rw1 or ar1 (which includes independent
noise) might be more appropriate.

> I would assume that if mgcv gives me basis functions (for a simple smoother
> with fixed df) that I can stick into the lm function, or obtain them via the
> jagam function and use them in JAGS with univariate normal priors, that I
> can also use them in INLA.

I would not assume that, unless the documentation explicitly says that
they can be interpreted and used in that way.

Finn

